==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|targe}}, either:
* from {{der|en|fro|targe}}, from {{der|en|frk|*targa||buckler}};
or
* from {{inh|en|ang|targa}} (masculine) and {{m|ang|targe}} (feminine);
both ultimately from {{der|en|non|targa||round shield}} from {{der|en|gem-pro|*targǭ||edge}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*derǵʰ-||fenced lot}}. Akin to {{cog|goh|zarga||side wall, rim}} ({{cog|de|Zarge||border, frame}}). However, the soft -g- seems to indicate a French origin.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/tɑːd͡ʒ/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/tɑɹd͡ʒ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[small]] [[shield#Noun|shield]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Andrew Brice|authorlink=Andrew Brice|title=Freedom: A Poem, Written in Time of Recess from the Rapacious Claws of Bailiffs, and Devouring Fangs of Goalers,{{nb...|by Andrew Brice, Printer. To which is Annexed, the Author’s Case.}}|location=Exon [Exeter, Devon]|publisher={{...|Printed by and for}} [T]he author,{{nb...|at his Printing-Office opposite to St. Stephen’s Church, in the High-street.}}|year=1730|page=80|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=XqpYAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA1-PA80|oclc=1103151136|passage=Or burley Hero &#91;{{w|Ajax the Great}}&#93; ''Sev'nfold '''Targe''''' who bore, / With Choler furibund, vindictive Steel / Plunging in Brutal Gore; [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Ivanhoe|volume=III|chapter=X|page=242|passage=The Jester wore his usual fantastic habit, but late accidents had led him to adopt a good cutting falchion, instead of his wooden sword, with a '''targe''' to match it; [...]}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[pendant#Noun|pendant]] or [[tassel]].

